\section{Variational Autoencoders}

\input{general_keras.tex}

We will implement a variational autoencoder in R using \texttt{keras3} and TensorFlow. Use the \href{https://github.com/MansMeg/IntroML/blob/master/additional_material/supplementary_r_files/variational_autoencoder_keras3.R}{feed-forward VAE example} as a starting point.

\emph{Note!} Training can take time. Run it once and reuse the fitted encoder and decoder for the remaining tasks. Keep the training output and figures for your report.

\begin{enumerate}
\item (1p) Start by loading the MNIST data in R. See the link above or previous assignments for details.
\item (1p) Now, implement a one-layer (encoder and decoder) feed-forward variational autoencoder with two latent dimensions. Both the encoder layer and the decoder layer should have 200 hidden units. You should end up with a variational autoencoder with roughly 310 000 - 320 000 parameters.
\examplebox{Sanity check}{
For a batch of $m$ images, the encoder should return an $m\times 2$ matrix of latent means. Decoding these should return an $m\times 784$ matrix with pixel values in $[0,1]$. Check a few images before full training.
}

\item Print the model and include it in your report.
\begin{enumerate}
\item (1p) How many weights (parameters) are used to compute $\mu$ and $\sigma^2$ for the latent variables?
\item (1p) \textbf{KL experiment.} The function below computes $D_{\mathrm{KL}}(q\Vert p)$, comparing the encoder distribution $q=N(\mu,\sigma^2)$ with the prior $p=N(\mu_0,\sigma_0^2)$. Both $\sigma$ and $\sigma_0$ are standard deviations. Keep the prior fixed at $(\mu_0,\sigma_0)=(0,1)$. Predict the ordering for $q$ with $(\mu,\sigma)=(0,1)$, $(2,1)$, and $(0,2)$, then calculate the KL values. Which $q$ is closest to the prior? Why would minimizing only KL fail to reconstruct different images?

\emph{Hint!} For this fixed prior, the function matches the one-dimensional KL term in the sampling layer.

The formula is
\[
D_{\mathrm{KL}}(q\Vert p)
= \log\frac{\sigma_0}{\sigma}
  + \frac{\sigma^2+(\mu-\mu_0)^2}{2\sigma_0^2} - \frac12.
\]
The function is available in \texttt{uuml} version 0.5.1 or later; reinstall the package using the general instructions if needed. Its implementation is printed below. Use \texttt{?kl\_normal} for documentation.
<<kl-normal-source,echo=FALSE,eval=TRUE,comment=NA,size="footnotesize">>=
cat("kl_normal <- ", paste(deparse(uuml::kl_normal), collapse = "\n"),
    "\n", sep = "")
@
<<kl-normal-example,eval=FALSE>>=
uuml::kl_normal(mu = -1, sigma = 0.5, mu_0 = 0, sigma_0 = 1)
@
\end{enumerate}
\item Before training, use the supplied \texttt{vae\_loss\_components()} scaffold to record the reconstruction, KL, and total losses. Train your variational autoencoder on MNIST for 50 epochs, then record them again using the same 100 images and the same sampled noise. The example stores these as \texttt{loss\_before} and \texttt{loss\_after}. Visualize the latent state for the different numbers.
\begin{enumerate}
\item (1p) How do you interpret this latent state?
\item (1p) What numbers are better represented by the latent state?
\item (1p) What number is less well represented by the latent state?
\item (1p) \textbf{Loss experiment.} Report the before/after loss table. Which term changed most? Do reconstruction and KL loss both need to decrease for their sum to improve? Explain using your results.
\end{enumerate}
\item (2p) Finally, encode all the 2:s in the MNIST test dataset to the latent state using your encoder. What is the mean of the digits "2" in the two latent dimensions? \emph{Hint!} See \texttt{y\_test} for the MNIST numbers. \emph{Note!} Do not retrain your VAE. Just use the one you have already trained.
\item (1p) Visualize this value of the latent state as a 28 by 28-pixel image using your decoder.
\end{enumerate}

\examplebox{Inspecting the latent grid}{
After training, use \texttt{uuml::plot\_vae\_grid(generator)} to inspect images decoded from a grid of two-dimensional latent coordinates. Here \texttt{generator} is your trained decoder, as named in the supplied example. The helper works with both the feed-forward and convolutional VAE examples and uses their existing weights. It does not train another model. Use \texttt{?plot\_vae\_grid} to see how to choose the coordinate grid and image dimensions.
}

% Reference image: earlier RMSprop run, seed 4711; current examples use Adam.
% 60,000 MNIST training images, 50 epochs, 315,988 parameters.
% Verified final train/validation losses: 159.49/160.07; keras3 1.5.1.
\examplebox{VAE reference output}{
\begin{minipage}[c]{0.30\linewidth}
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figs/assignment7_vae_reference.png}
\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}[c]{0.66\linewidth}
Illustrative decoder grid from an earlier training run of the feed-forward VAE after 50 epochs on MNIST. Each cell decodes a different latent coordinate. Some digits may look blurred or blended. Your arrangement can differ because training is stochastic and the latent axes have no fixed interpretation.
\end{minipage}
}

\section{Topic Models}

We will now analyze the classical book Pride and Prejudice by Jane Austen using a probabilistic topic model. If you have not read the book, \href{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pride_and_Prejudice}{\textbf{here}} you can read up on the story of this classical book.

We will use a Gibbs sampler to estimate ten different topics occurring in Pride and Prejudice and study where they occur. A tokenized version of the book and a \texttt{data.frame} with stopwords can be loaded as follows:

<<message=FALSE>>=
library(uuml)
library(dplyr)
library(tidytext)
data("pride_and_prejudice")
data("stopwords")
@


\newpage

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Setup (ungraded).} Remove stopwords (common English words without much semantic information):
<<>>=
pap <- pride_and_prejudice
pap <- anti_join(pap, y = stopwords[stopwords$lexicon == "snowball",])
@

\item \textbf{Setup (ungraded).} Remove words that occur at most five times:
<<>>=
word_freq <- table(pap$word)
rare_words <- data.frame(word = names(word_freq[word_freq <= 5]),
                         stringsAsFactors = FALSE)
pap <- anti_join(pap, y = rare_words)
@
\item (1p) The supplied code builds a document-term matrix, treating each paragraph as a document. Report the number of documents and terms (word types).
<<eval=FALSE>>=
library(tm)
crp <- aggregate(pap$word, by = list(pap$paragraph),
                 FUN = paste0, collapse = " ")
names(crp) <- c("paragraph", "text")
s <- SimpleCorpus(VectorSource(crp$text))
m <- DocumentTermMatrix(s)
@
\item \textbf{Setup (ungraded).} Fit a topic model with ten topics using Gibbs sampling for 2000 iterations and the fixed seed below. Fit the model once and reuse the fitted model \texttt{tm} for the likelihood trace, topic interpretation, and paragraph plots.
<<eval=FALSE>>=
library(topicmodels)
K <- 10
# Note: delta is beta in Griffith and Steyvers (2004) notation.
control <- list(keep = 1, delta = 0.1, alpha = 1, iter = 2000,
                seed = 4711)
tm <- LDA(m, k = K, method = "Gibbs", control)
@
\item \textbf{Setup (ungraded).} Extract $\Theta$, $\Phi$, and the log-likelihood values using the supplied code. This is the parameter notation used in \citet{griffiths2004finding}.
<<eval=FALSE>>=
library(uuml)
lls <- extract_log_liks(tm)
theta <- extract_theta(tm)
phi <- extract_phi(tm)
@
\newpage

\item (2p) Plot the log-likelihood against Gibbs iterations. Does it settle into a roughly stable range, or does a persistent trend remain? Support your assessment using the plot.

\emph{Hint!} Fluctuations are normal in Gibbs sampling. Look for changes in the typical level rather than expecting a smooth or steadily increasing curve. A single likelihood trace cannot establish convergence of all model parameters.

\item (2p) Inspect the 20 top words for each topic and choose two whose words seem to belong together. For each chosen topic, inspect one or two paragraphs where its estimated topic proportion is high. Include the two word lists, suggest an informative label for each topic, and explain each label using the top words and paragraph content.

\emph{Hint!} Set \texttt{topic} to a chosen topic number. Use \texttt{crp\$paragraph} to map rows of \texttt{theta} back to the original text.
<<topic_paragraph_hint,eval=FALSE>>=
topic <- 1
paragraph_ids <- crp$paragraph[
  head(order(theta[, topic], decreasing = TRUE), 2)]
pride_and_prejudice |>
  filter(paragraph %in% paragraph_ids) |>
  group_by(paragraph) |>
  summarise(text = paste(word, collapse = " "), .groups = "drop")
@
\item (2p) Visualize these two topics evolve over the paragraphs in the books by plotting the $\theta$ parameters for that topic over time (paragraphs) in the book. Think of this as the time-line of the book. On the y-axis, you should plot $\theta_i$ for your chosen topic $i$ and the x-axis should be the paragraph number (first paragraph has number 1 and so forth).
\item (2p) How do these two chosen topics evolve over the course in the book? If you want, you can take a rolling mean of the theta parameters to more easily show the changes in the topic over the book. \emph{Hint!} Here \texttt{zoo::rollmean()} might be a good function to use.
\end{enumerate}

