\section{* Prompt engineering with large language models}

You can choose to either do this task or the task above to get VG on this assignment.

\input{distinction_task.tex}

For this task, choose either \textbf{OpenAI} or \textbf{Anthropic (Claude)}. You only need a developer account and an API key with your chosen provider. Follow the \href{https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/quickstart}{OpenAI API quickstart} or the \href{https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/get-started}{Claude API quickstart}. API billing is separate from consumer chat subscriptions; check the current \href{https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/pricing}{OpenAI API prices} or \href{https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing}{Claude API prices} before running your experiments.

Install \texttt{ellmer} from CRAN and open your personal \texttt{.Renviron} file:
<<ellmer-install,eval=FALSE>>=
install.packages("ellmer")
file.edit(path.expand("~/.Renviron"))
@
Add \emph{only the line for your chosen provider}, replacing the placeholder with your own API key, then save the file and restart R:
\begin{itemize}
\item OpenAI: \verb|OPENAI_API_KEY=YOUR_API_KEY|
\item Anthropic: \verb|ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=YOUR_API_KEY|
\end{itemize}
\texttt{ellmer} reads the corresponding environment variable automatically. Select your provider below; the check confirms that a key has been set without printing it:
<<ellmer-key-check,eval=FALSE>>=
library(ellmer)
provider <- "openai"  # Change to "anthropic" for Claude.
key_name <- switch(provider,
  openai = "OPENAI_API_KEY",
  anthropic = "ANTHROPIC_API_KEY",
  stop("Choose 'openai' or 'anthropic'.")
)
stopifnot(nzchar(Sys.getenv(key_name)))
@
\textcolor{red}{\emph{Warning:}} Keep your API key private. Do not put it in your R scripts or report, and do not upload your \texttt{.Renviron} file to Github, public Colab notebooks, or other shared locations.

\textbf{Choosing the baseline model.} Start with the cheapest currently available general-purpose text model that you can access through your chosen provider's API. Define ``cheapest'' using the published standard price for 1,000 input tokens and 1,000 output tokens, excluding caching and batch discounts. Consult the \href{https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models}{OpenAI model list} or \href{https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/models/overview}{Claude model list} and the corresponding pricing page. Use this baseline for the initial hallucination experiment and the few-shot classification task below.

Replace \texttt{YOUR\_MODEL\_ID} with its exact model ID. Always specify a model explicitly because \texttt{ellmer}'s defaults can change.
<<ellmer-call,eval=FALSE>>=
model_id <- "YOUR_MODEL_ID"  # Replace with your baseline model ID.

new_chat <- function(model = model_id) {
  if (provider == "openai") {
    chat_openai(model = model, echo = "none")
  } else {
    chat_anthropic(model = model, echo = "none", cache = "none")
  }
}

ask_llm <- function(prompt, model = model_id) {
  chat <- new_chat(model)
  as.character(chat$chat(prompt))
}

response <- ask_llm("Who is Gustav Vasa? Give a short answer.")
cat(response)
@
An illustrative response is shown below; the actual wording can differ.
<<echo=FALSE,eval=TRUE>>=
cat("Gustav Vasa was a Swedish king who led a successful rebellion
against Danish rule and became the founder of modern Sweden in
the 16th century.")
@
Each call to \texttt{ask\_llm()} starts a fresh conversation. Use it for independent prompts and for each quote in the classification task below, so earlier quotes and responses do not affect later predictions.

For a deliberate multi-turn experiment, create one chat object and reuse it. Here, the second prompt includes the first exchange as context:
<<ellmer-conversation,eval=FALSE>>=
chat <- new_chat()
first_response <- chat$chat("Who is Gustav Vasa? Give a short answer.")
follow_up <- chat$chat("Which century did he live in?")
cat(follow_up)
@
See \texttt{?chat\_openai}, \texttt{?chat\_anthropic}, and the \href{https://ellmer.tidyverse.org/}{ellmer documentation} for further details. If a request reports insufficient quota, check your chosen provider's billing and available API credits.


\newpage
\subsection{Make a language model hallucinate}

We are now going to understand how and when transformer-based decoder models 'hallucinate', i.e. when the models give factually incorrect information. See \citet[Section 7.1.2]{zhao2023survey} for an introduction and \citet{huang2023survey} for details on hallucinations in LLMs.

Create three different hallucinations with three different strategies using your baseline model (i.e. it is not enough to use the same prompt for three incorrect generations). For each generated hallucination, report:

\begin{enumerate}
\item The full prompt, including the conversation history for a multi-turn experiment, and the generation settings used. Record a seed if the API supports one; otherwise state that it is unavailable. You may experiment with longer conversations and generation settings.
\item The response from the LLM.
\item Why the response is factually incorrect and whether it is an intrinsic or extrinsic hallucination.
\item The strategy you used and your analysis of why the hallucination happened.
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Comparing with a stronger model.} Select a second model from the \emph{same provider} that the provider describes as more capable than your baseline. Repeat the same prompts using this stronger model, starting a fresh conversation for each independent test. For a multi-turn test, replay the same sequence of user prompts in a new conversation with the stronger model. Report both transcripts.

For a single-prompt test, set \texttt{stronger\_model\_id} to the stronger model's exact ID and use \texttt{ask\_llm(prompt, model = stronger\_model\_id)}. For a multi-turn test, start with \texttt{new\_chat(model = stronger\_model\_id)}.

Report your provider, both exact model IDs, the date of your experiments, and the published input and output token prices, with a link to the pricing page. Explain why you chose the stronger model. Then discuss:
\begin{enumerate}
\item The stronger model's response to each test. Is it still factually incorrect? Does it express uncertainty or correct the mistaken premise? If you find no hallucinations in these responses, state that.
\item How factual accuracy, hallucinations, and uncertainty differ between the two models. Does the stronger model perform better on your examples, and would the difference justify its higher cost?
\end{enumerate}


\newpage
\subsection{In-Context Learning for few-shot classification}

In-context learning is the idea to use large language models to complete tasks in a few-shot or zero-shot way. In this task we are going to try to classify political quotes in Swedish to weather they belong to the social democratic party manifesto or the conservative party (Moderaterna) manifesto.

\emph{Note!} This data is included in \texttt{uuml} version 0.4.0 and later.

<<eval=TRUE>>=
library(uuml)
data("pc_test")
data("pc_train")
@

Below is an example on how you can combine the data to a text string that can be combined to be used in a prompt. Note, you need to develop further on this.

<<eval=FALSE>>=
example_string <- paste0(pc_train$quote,
                         ": ",
                         pc_train$party, collapse = "\n\n")
cat(example_string)
@
\begin{exampleblock}{Prompt formatting}
<<prompt_formatting_example,eval=FALSE>>=
prompt <- paste(
  "Classify the quote. Return only the party label.",
  paste("Examples:", example_string, sep = "\n"),
  paste("Quote to classify:", pc_test$quote[1], sep = "\n"),
  sep = "\n\n"
)
cat(prompt)
@
\texttt{"\textbackslash n"} starts a new line; \texttt{"\textbackslash n\textbackslash n"} separates blocks with a blank line. Use \texttt{cat(prompt)} to inspect the formatting before sending it.
\end{exampleblock}

Use the techniques from \cite[][Section 6.1-6.3]{zhao2023survey} to generate as good prompts as possible to classify weather a quote is from the social democratic or the conservative manifesto.


\begin{enumerate}
\item Start with zero-shot learning, ie only prompt the LLM without any demonstrations. Test to classify the quotes in the test set.
\item Test how far you can get by using better prompt descriptions to classify the quotes. Try to get responses as classes from the model.
\item Now add demonstrations by adding examples from the training set. Does that improve the accuracy on the testset?
\item Try at least three different strategies from \cite[][Section 6.1-6.3]{zhao2023survey} to improve the quality. Discuss your conclusions.
\end{enumerate}

